227 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 71 0 R/E(The Australian Competition and Consumer Commission has previously raised concerns about hearing-aid sales practices, including commissions, sales targets, preferred-brand arrangements and recommendations for more expensive products.)/Pg 17 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

228 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 71 0 R/E(That does not mean a more expensive hearing aid is the wrong choice. It means you should compare your options and understand exactly what you are paying for.)/Pg 17 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

229 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 71 0 R/E(When comparing quotes, look for the exact brand, model, technology level, fitting, follow-up support, warranty and trial period. Labels such as \215premium\216, \215gold\216 or \215platinum\216 mean very little without the actual product details.)/Pg 17 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

230 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 71 0 R/T(What Are You Paying More For?)/E(What Are You Paying More For?)/Pg 17 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

231 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 71 0 R/E(Higher technology levels are usually about more sophisticated processing, not simply making sound louder.)/Pg 17 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

232 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 71 0 R/E(Paying more may give you better support for speech in noise, automatic adjustments, personalisation and complex listening environments.)/Pg 17 0 R/K[8]>>
